ave you  an interesting dimension seeing john  david  sort of center that film and carry the  audience with him  it was impossible for me to get get him  out of my head at that point because i  tried to write without actors in mind  but after i'd seen him in that film and  seen how as i say he  carried us with him on on that journey  it was very tough for me to to not think  of him while i was writing after that  and  and then i met him when i finished the  script and creatively  you know we just we just clicked i think  he has  this warmth and accessibility that's  extremely important  for the protagonist because what we're  trying to do there is create an iconic  presence at the heart of the film and  it's  it's it's something you can't approach  directly you can't  try and find an actor who will just be  an icon of  you know spy movies or whatever you have  to approach it in a different way and  look at  who the character needs to be and for us  and john dave and i were  really on the same page it it was about  removing the cynicism that these  characters are usually drenched in  because there's a paradox there you know  if you're looking at a character who  will lay down his life for his fellow  man or for an ideal or for an abstract  principle which is very often what these  characters are  saying they're prepared to do to us it  felt that that had to  encompass a degree of faith in in  humanity and  and a warmth and an uh feeling of  empathy  uh towards people around him and so  balancing that  with this this sort of um get things  done  there's this kind of ruthlessness as far  as the task at hand  i think he found a way to reconcile  those two  those two very different impulses and  and i think it  presents a really remarkable aspect to  the character i can feel the tender  affection between the conversation  attitude of protagonist and neil  did you explain to john and robert the  special relationship  between the two characters um  yeah i mean it's i think a lot of it was  there on the page for them actually  um but then once you you cast the film  uh because their relationship you know  as you watch the film it becomes  more and more important to the story i  suppose you'd say um  you know there are some ambiguities to a  relationship and then and then there's a  there's a camaraderie that develops it's  very important um  a lot of that was was indicated on the  page  but once you finish a script you then  look around and say okay who can play  these parts  you put people together um  and then you know you see the chemistry  that develops and and these guys  you know john david and rob pattinson um  you know they just connected and it  really really  for me it just just worked with what we  were trying to do with with the story  it's really fun to watch these guys work  together and it's  i think it's really fun to watch the the  characters interact in the movie  once you know how do characters return  to their original timelines  after traveling backwards um there's  obviously a lot going on with um  you know characters moving different  timelines i assume the question is sort  of  um you know yeah how do they do they go  back to their original timeline or do  they stay on that timeline  well the question slightly  misunderstands  the rules um their timeline is their  timeline  and through the rules of inversion it it  can be inverted and they can move  backwards through time they can then  reinvert  using a turn style and then start moving  forwards in time but they will  continue to be uh on that timeline  right so it's also easier to draw a  diagram it's also one timeline it's  one uh the best way to think of it and  and this  relates to the earlier question is if  you look at john david's experience and  you follow him through the entire story  yeah that's a single timeline that  you're following  but that timeline can move both forwards  and backwards and that's  where i was interested in these this  this rule set  uh because it's quite distinct from  time travel per se or time travel movies  where multiple realities or something  like that exactly  and they refer to the possibility of  multiple realities but the  terms of the storytelling are pretty  tight in that you follow  this character and you're looking over  shoulder the whole way and you're  you're experiencing his timeline on how  that can meander forwards and backwards  you know time is uh  is something that we can tell you have a  fascination with  over a number of films where do you  think that comes from where does that  fascination and interest and intrigue  come from  well i'd like to say i'm fascinated by  time because i've always lived in it  and it's a big glimpse but there's  real truth to that though because we we  are all obviously living in time and  living with time and it's one of the  things that  causes us it's one of the biggest  influences on our lives the biggest  influence probably and yet  we're unable to fully understand it  we're unable to really grasp it or even  explain what it is quite frankly  and that's fascinating to me it's one of  the great enigmas that  that we all have to deal with every  second of our day  and the relationship between that  subjective experience of time and the  way  the ways in which we experience time  changing  at different points in our lives  different points in our day  the relationship between that subjective  experience and  the mechanism of cinema and how shot  following shot following shot  builds up a narrative experience in the  minds of the audience you sit there in a  cinema and you you receive a story in  roughly two hours  but the time span of that story can be  it could be  minutes to centuries to millennia you  know it's  it's it's all possible and that's a  fascinating relationship and  the mechanism whereby that relationship  is exploited or takes place to me is  fascinating and i like the idea of  exposing that mechanism and involving  the audience in you know  i suppose you'd say a discussion of that  mechanism just trying to make them  experience or notice  the way in which a movie is making them  feel for me tennis first and foremost  action film and the science fiction  component  gave me a reason to portray these action  set pieces that we've all perhaps  you know seen in other movies you know  whether it's a car chase or a  plane crash or circle to mount it on a  large scale but  look at it in a different way look at it  through a different lens and  for me as an audience member that's just  what i'm always looking for some kind of  freshness to things  so i want to enjoy a large-scale action  movie i want to go on that thrill  ride but i want something new out of it  i want i want to  look at it in a way that i haven't  before i use genre to try  and play with audience expectations i  don't mean play within a sort of  toying sense but in a sense of what can  a particular genre the conventions of a  genre what advantage is going to give  you with  audience expectation and in dealing with  something as abstract and slippery as  time and  inversion of time having the the solid  structure of the spy film with the  expectations that that  presents to the audience and the  familiarity the familiar elements that  that gives the audience to hold on to  that lets me try and take the audience  places they've never been before  i mean 10 years ago with inception we  try to do that using the conventions of  the heist film  to explore dreams and dreams within  dreams and and so forth  and i think really this is you know for  me is a very similar thing i'm taking  the  the spy film genre and using it to  address  uh ideas of of time uh were there  multiple endings you thought of using  and had to decide from if so what were  some of them i know you know often the  ending for the film you'll think of  pretty early in the process of kind of  where you want to go but did you  were there other scenes or you know not  deleted scenes but sort of other  concepts of the end of the film i mean  no  the simple answer is uh i don't go into  a project without  knowing the ending because i think the  ending is uh  it's the most important part of feature  film i think  um i think that's one of the the things  that keeps feature films very distinct  from television  because in a feature film you know you  can have a you know  a four-hour movie and the last five  minutes can let it down and then  for the audience the whole experience  gets thrown out the window um  it's it's it's like the the end of an  anecdote or the punchline to a joke like  it  it justifies everything you've seen so i  always come up with the  the ending early on and and hopefully  it's something that  uh you know it has to be something that  emerges really specifically from the  story situation that you're examining so  no i don't sort of look at a  mixed bag of potential endings and then  sift through them  um the only time i've ever in in my work  i've ever had sort of had  as it were multiple endings is when i  made insomnia uh for warner brothers  years ago they  um they were very insistent that i shoot  an alternative ending  uh i don't want to give away the ending  in the film because a lot of people  won't have seen it and maybe they'll get  to enjoy it but uh  they wanted me to shoot two different  endings and i agreed to do that  and uh fortunately because i'd shot this  alternate ending they never asked to see  it they knew it was in the care  and then they let me you know test  screen the the ending we all we all  loved and we went we went with that but  uh  that's really the only time i've been in  a situation where there was possibly  going to be an  alternate ending that's not generally  something i'm comfortable with at all  um speaking of the ending another  question from twitter  uh did you make a casablanca reference  in neil's last  scene louie  i think this is the beginning of a  beautiful friendship  for me i think this is the end of a  beautiful friendship but for me it's  just the beginning  well i don't want to have it i don't  want to have any spoilers for people who  haven't seen the film  yes there's very much a casper reference  in the last scene  excellent hi everyone thank you all for  watching  if you'd like to see more advice from  christopher nolan look into the  description of this video  where you will find the link to 10 tips  from his other films  also in the description there is a link  that will lead you  to our patreon page where you can  support this channel  and watch 5 extra screenwriting and  directing tips from christopher nolan  among with other videos that we don't  share anywhere else  see you all next week have there been  times working on a script  that you thought that you'd never be  able to complete it you have that  writer's block  i every day on the script uh yeah  definitely i mean it's something any  writer deals with  a lot every problem always seems  insurmountable the  the cruel thing the cruel trick that  your mind plays on you when you when  you're writing is  you sort of go to bed at night  thinking oh i've got the whole thing  worked out and i can write it no problem  tomorrow and then you sit down and  you're like hang on  it seemed so simple till i started  writing it  and you're often getting stuck i mean i  think the longest i was stuck  the longest i was stuck was on inception  i you know i got to page  80 or so you know i had two acts of it  and i was stuck for about  i mean 10 years or so you know i mean i  did other things as well i wasn't  sitting here the typewriter all work and  no play yeah it wasn't that  but you know i was i was just jammed on  it uh  and then i had a breakthrough uh and  the old cliche of if you're having  trouble  if there's something wrong with the  third act it's actually wrong with the  first and second act you know that's  where you really need to do the work  i have always found that to be true like  if you if you're absolutely stuck  you know on that third act it probably  means that some of the ground work you  laid in the first two acts  is flawed somehow and with inception  that was definitely the case and i went  back and  with a new take on it and it uh it then  got finished pretty rapidly  you
 

